--TEST--
An empty topic name is refused by both `Core\Topic` members, and refused before either asks whether this program is a connection — `rule:core-classes/topic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A name is a topic's whole identity, and the empty one is the name no
// publisher can mean and no subscriber can be reached by. It is wrong wherever
// it is written, which is why it is the *call* that is reported rather than the
// host: a program that ran this outside a connection has two things wrong with
// it, and the one it can fix from the line it wrote is this one.
try {
    Core\Topic::subscribe("");
    echo "subscribed\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Topic::unsubscribe("");
    echo "unsubscribed\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The order is what this case pins, and a name built at run time is where it
// shows: neither call above is on a connection, so a member that asked about
// the host first would answer both of them with the same sentence and this one
// with a third. Concatenating to nothing is how an empty name is actually
// reached -- nobody writes the literal.
var $room = "";
try {
    Core\Topic::subscribe("" . $room);
    echo "subscribed\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
`Core\Topic::subscribe` needs a topic name and was given an empty one
`Core\Topic::unsubscribe` needs a topic name and was given an empty one
`Core\Topic::subscribe` needs a topic name and was given an empty one
